WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.916
Studies of human-robot interaction show that a
robot's motion changes how nearby people walk,

00:00:05.996 --> 00:00:12.492
and these effects are strongest when the robot and
pedestrians share the same space at close distance.

00:00:12.572 --> 00:00:14.039
To study and model that,

00:00:14.119 --> 00:00:18.359
we need data collected while a robot actually moves
through crowds,

00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:20.874
not from a camera bolted to a building.

00:00:20.954 --> 00:00:27.256
Earlier robot datasets came close but each had a
gap: STCrowd kept its sensors at a fixed position,

00:00:27.336 --> 00:00:28.804
so scenes barely varied,

00:00:28.884 --> 00:00:36.025
and JRDB did not organize its data into
trajectories and did not fully synchronize its multiple sensors in time,

00:00:36.105 --> 00:00:37.765
which limits sensor fusion.

00:00:37.845 --> 00:00:40.860
SiT is designed specifically to fill these gaps.
